WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.425 --> 00:00:15.309
and his men left the Island of the Cyclops, not realizing that Polyphemus had asked his father Poseidon

2
00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:15.869
to curse them.

3
00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:21.753
Unlike mortal men, the gods can be quite patient in their revenge, so Poseidon allowed Odysseus and his

4
00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:23.275
men to sail where the wind took them.

5
00:00:24.035 --> 00:00:27.858
After several days of sailing they came to a strange island that seemed to be floating on the

6
00:00:27.918 --> 00:00:29.459
sea, like a large vessel.

7
00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:33.625
The island was inhabited, because there was a large villa with a windswept pavilion.

8
00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:36.746
The craftsmanship of the villa and the pavilion were incredible.

9
00:00:36.766 --> 00:00:41.967
"This place is too strange and too beautiful to be the work of mortal men," said Odysseus to

10
00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:42.387
his crew.

11
00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:47.068
"Let us go there cautiously, because we do not want to make the gods or people who live

12
00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:47.648
there angry."

13
00:00:48.409 --> 00:00:52.130
His crew agreed, and they drew lots to see who among them would accompany Odysseus.

14
00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.491
From his own portion of the treasure, Odysseus chose a gift to bring their host.

15
00:00:57.291 --> 00:01:00.752
It was a broach made of gold, silver, and jewels in the shape of a butterfly.

16
00:01:01.128 --> 00:01:04.829
"In beauty there is nothing finer than this broach," said Odysseus to his men.

17
00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:09.890
"I chose this as a gift for my wife, but a beautiful present is worthless so far from

18
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.712
home. I hope its beauty will inspire the god or goddess who will be our host to hasten

19
00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:15.252
our journey."

20
00:01:15.992 --> 00:01:19.553
The men nodded and appreciated the sacrifice that their leader was making for all of them.

21
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:24.534
Cleanliness is next to godliness, so Odysseus and the men chosen to go with him bathed in the

22
00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:25.855
ocean before leaving the ship.

23
00:01:26.815 --> 00:01:30.036
The villa and pavilion were located on a steep mountain that overlooked the beach.

24
00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:34.557
They could tell that the trail up to the villa was seldom traveled because it was overgrown with

25
00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:35.457
grass and weeds.

26
00:01:36.058 --> 00:01:39.799
As Odysseus and his men approached the top of the mountain they thought they could hear someone or

27
00:01:39.859 --> 00:01:40.579
something whistling.

28
00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:45.500
But when they entered the pavilion there was no one; only the four winds chasing themselves among the

29
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.601
columns of the pavilion.

30
00:01:47.461 --> 00:01:50.782
From the villa, Odysseus and his men heard what sounded like a party.

31
00:01:51.322 --> 00:01:53.083
There was shouting, singing, and much laughter.

32
00:01:53.103 --> 00:01:57.024
"I think our host is throwing quite a party," said Odysseus to his men.

33
00:01:57.788 --> 00:02:01.849
"If fate has not doomed us to die here upon this windswept rock, I think we shall enjoy

34
00:02:01.909 --> 00:02:05.530
ourselves." Odysseus and his men eagerly went to the door of the villa.

35
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:10.452
But before Odysseus could knock, the four winds came rushing past, and blew the door wide open.

36
00:02:10.472 --> 00:02:14.013
"Quiet!" shouted a tall man with windswept silver hair.

37
00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.994
"The winds are my pets, and they have brought me news of visitors.

38
00:02:18.614 --> 00:02:20.595
Let us see who has come to the island of the winds."

39
00:02:21.435 --> 00:02:26.337
Odysseus bowed low and his men did likewise, "Great and noble immortal, I am Odysseus.

40
00:02:26.577 --> 00:02:28.438
I have brought you a token of our goodwill."

41
00:02:29.299 --> 00:02:33.622
From beneath his cloak, he took out the wondrous jewel-encrusted butterfly and gave it to the god.

42
00:02:33.642 --> 00:02:37.284
"My men and I are returning from our victorious triumph at Troy."

43
00:02:37.304 --> 00:02:39.066
"Well met, Odysseus.

44
00:02:39.646 --> 00:02:42.948
I am Aeolus, the keeper of the winds, and they are my children.

45
00:02:43.389 --> 00:02:48.392
This is indeed a broach of the finest craftmanship; it is, I think, too fine to be worn.

46
00:02:48.772 --> 00:02:49.693
I will set it free."

47
00:02:50.273 --> 00:02:53.696
Aeolus blew on the butterfly, and it spread its wings and fluttered around the room.

48
00:02:54.804 --> 00:02:58.626
"Thank you for your gift, and please do not think me rude, but I would like to make

49
00:02:58.666 --> 00:02:59.486
a request of you.

50
00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:03.088
I grow weary of the conversation and songs of my children.

51
00:03:03.608 --> 00:03:07.990
If you tell me the story of your triumph, I will give the wayward voyager a most precious

52
00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:12.672
gift." Odysseus and his men gladly retold their tale of battles won and battles lost.

53
00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:18.654
Aeolus and his children were greatly entertained, and Aeolus took Odysseus aside and gave him a large, leather

54
00:03:18.694 --> 00:03:23.625
bound sack saying, "Tie this sack to the mast of your ship, for I have captured all the

55
00:03:23.665 --> 00:03:26.467
winds except for the west one which will take you home.

56
00:03:27.128 --> 00:03:30.050
The west wind will chase her friends and take your ship home.

57
00:03:30.531 --> 00:03:34.714
Do not open the sack until you are home, or the other winds will drive you away in

58
00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:38.057
anger." Odysseus thanked Aeolus, and did as he said.

59
00:03:38.577 --> 00:03:41.760
He was so happy to be finally on his way home that he stood at the wheel of

60
00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.501
his ship for four days and four nights.

61
00:03:44.022 --> 00:03:48.165
On the morning of the fifth day he and his men came within sight of Ithaca, and home.

62
00:03:48.986 --> 00:03:51.388
Exhausted, Odysseus fell asleep where he stood.

63
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.263
While Odysseus had been sailing, Poseidon had been whispering to the crew, "Odysseus isn't going to share the

64
00:03:58.323 --> 00:03:59.824
treasure that Aeolus gave to him.

65
00:04:00.364 --> 00:04:02.785
Open the bag and divide it fairly among yourselves."

66
00:04:03.585 --> 00:04:08.707
While Odysseus was awake no man dared to open the bag, but once Odysseus was asleep, the men

67
00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:12.609
in all the ships stared greedily at the large sack tied to the mast of Odysseus' ship.

68
00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.891
One of the men climbed up the mast and unfastened the sack's leather strap.

69
00:04:17.831 --> 00:04:18.852
The roar was deafening.

70
00:04:19.296 --> 00:04:23.197
The three imprisoned winds rushed out of the sack, and Poseidon filled their anger with lies.

71
00:04:23.637 --> 00:04:27.959
Soon the winds were chasing Odysseus and his ships across the sea and straight back to Aeolus' island.

72
00:04:28.819 --> 00:04:33.580
After Aeolus heard the winds' tale, he knew that Odysseus had angered Poseidon and he refused to help

73
00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.860
Odysseus a second time.

74
00:04:35.681 --> 00:04:40.502
Odysseus and his men left the floating island, dreading Poseidon's anger and the fate that awaited them.

75
00:04:41.322 --> 00:04:43.963
The angry winds continued to plague Odysseus and his men.

76
00:04:44.503 --> 00:04:46.984
The winds drove them to an island inhabited by giants.

77
00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.961
Odysseus and his men made for the safety of a harbor between two steep cliffs.

78
00:04:51.701 --> 00:04:54.522
Once they were safely within the harbor the winds left them alone.

79
00:04:55.042 --> 00:04:55.922
But it was a trap.

80
00:04:56.783 --> 00:04:59.303
Poseidon had warned the giants of Odysseus' approach.

81
00:05:00.184 --> 00:05:04.025
From the cliffs, the giants hurled massive boulders of granite at Odysseus and his ships.

82
00:05:05.025 --> 00:05:08.206
Soon the boulders were falling like rain, smashing and sinking his ships.

83
00:05:08.866 --> 00:05:12.507
Odysseus ordered his men to flee, but there were too many boulders falling.

84
00:05:13.007 --> 00:05:16.488
And Odysseus watched as each of his ships sank and his men died.

85
00:05:17.032 --> 00:05:19.534
Odysseus' ship, however, was curiously protected.

86
00:05:20.254 --> 00:05:24.657
Athena had extended the corner of her great shield to protect Odysseus from the death that was happening

87
00:05:24.697 --> 00:05:25.318
all around him.

88
00:05:25.878 --> 00:05:29.200
He and his men escaped, but they cried bitterly for the friends they had lost.

89
00:05:30.001 --> 00:05:33.943
They left the land of the giants and sailed knowing that fate had spared them, but not knowing

90
00:05:33.983 --> 00:05:34.644
for how long.
